Linear vectorization is supported on backends that declare ``"vectorize-linear"``
in ``supported_features`` (currently C and Go), independent of ``--arch``. Other
backends reject both the command-line option and the configuration.

With ``--vectorize-linear`` (or ``re2c:vectorize:linear`` configuration) |re2c|
looks for *linear chains* in the DFA: sequences of states where every state has
exactly one single-character transition that continues the chain. Such chains
appear when the input is dispatched on long fixed strings, for example HTTP
header names, tag or attribute names, or file signatures. At the first state of
a chain the generated lexer assembles the next 2, 4 or 8 code units into a
little-endian word and compares it with the packed characters on the chain. If
they are equal, it advances the cursor and jumps to the last state of the chain;
otherwise it continues with the ordinary one-character dispatch. The fast path
is taken only when the ordinary dispatch would follow the same transitions, so
the matched rule, the match length and the cursor are the same as without the
option.

The option can help when the lexer spends its time on long literals that are
often matched to the end, for example when classifying isolated known header
names along linear paths. It is not a general keyword-recognition optimization:
with an identifier rule such as ``[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*``, every prefix of a
keyword is an accepting state, and the chain stops there. On short tokens or
unknown names that rarely match a literal, the extra comparison may cost more
than it saves.

There is no universal speedup for C lexers using ``YYFILL``. Comparing isolated
names with a refilled stream that also scans header values changes several
factors at once. Benchmark the actual lexer, input distribution and buffer
contract rather than assuming that fewer character comparisons imply lower
running time.

Control flow is emitted entirely through |re2c|'s existing IR constructs:
``code:if_then_else`` for the bounds guard and word comparison,
``code:yylessthan`` for the remaining-length check, ``code:yyshift`` for the
cursor advance (by ``n - 1`` code units in default lazy-skip mode because the
target state consumes one unit on entry, or ``n`` code units with
``--eager-skip``), and standard state jumps. The atomic wide-read and literal
expressions are defined in the syntax file (see the section about syntax files):

``code:yypeek2``, ``code:yypeek4``, ``code:yypeek8``
    Expressions that yield the unsigned 2-byte, 4-byte or 8-byte value at the
    current input position, where the code unit at offset ``k`` occupies bits
    ``8*k`` to ``8*k+7``. The packed literal compared against uses the same
    layout, so the comparison does not depend on the byte order of the host.
    Supported variables: ``YYPEEK2`` (or ``YYPEEK4``, ``YYPEEK8``), ``YYINPUT``,
    ``YYCURSOR``, ``yyrecord``.

``code:hex_literal``
    Packed hexadecimal literal compared against the wide read. The generator
    supplies the hexadecimal value in ``val``, and the conditional ``.wide``
    (true for 8-byte literals) lets the template choose an integer suffix: the
    C syntax file appends ``u`` or ``ull`` while Go uses an unsuffixed literal.

The C and Go syntax files define ``code:yypeek2``, ``code:yypeek4``,
``code:yypeek8`` and ``code:hex_literal`` for the ``simple``, ``record`` and
``generic`` APIs:

- In the ``simple`` and ``record`` APIs, the default C templates assemble
  ``(unsigned char)(YYCURSOR)[k]`` into ``unsigned int`` (widths 2 and 4) or
  ``unsigned long long`` (width 8, C99 / C++11; for C90 or C++98 override the
  templates in a custom syntax file or leave the option disabled), and the
  default Go templates assemble ``uint8(YYINPUT[YYCURSOR:][k])`` into
  ``uint16``, ``uint32`` or ``uint64``. Combining these byte-shift reads into a
  single machine load depends on the compiler, optimization flags and
  surrounding code; in goto/label and loop/switch modes the fast path is emitted
  before the scalar peek to help load fusion.
- With ``--api generic``, wide reads invoke ``re2c:YYPEEK2``, ``re2c:YYPEEK4``
  and ``re2c:YYPEEK8``, bounds checks use ``YYLESSTHAN``, and cursor advancement
  uses ``YYSHIFT``; no ``YYCURSOR``, ``YYLIMIT`` or ``YYINPUT`` identifier is
  emitted in generic API output.

Both with and without ``YYFILL``, every wide read is guarded by
``!(<code:yylessthan(n)>)`` so that a ``YYFILL`` which does not provide the
requested input (for example a no-op ``YYFILL`` in a sentinel lexer without
``YYMAXFILL`` padding) cannot read past the end of the input: when fewer than
``n`` code units remain, the fast path is skipped and the ordinary scalar
dispatch runs instead. Across all backends supporting linear vectorization (both
C and Go), if ``YYFILL`` or its check is disabled, the bounds check primitive
must be explicitly configured (for example, ``re2c:YYLIMIT = YYLIMIT;`` in C or
``re2c:YYLIMIT = "len(yyinput)";`` in Go for the simple API,
``re2c:YYLIMIT = "yyrecord->yylimit";`` for a record field, or
``re2c:YYLESSTHAN = YYLESSTHAN;`` for the generic API); otherwise linear
vectorization is disabled with a warning so that sentinel-only lexers require no
``YYLIMIT`` or ``YYLESSTHAN`` declaration.

The optimization applies only to target encodings with one-byte code units,
including ASCII and UTF-8. For wider target encodings, or when ``re2c:eof`` is
configured (where ``YYFILL`` does not reserve input in advance), it is disabled
with a warning controlled by ``-Wvectorize-linear-disabled``. An undefined
``code:yyshift``, ``code:yylessthan`` or ``code:hex_literal``, or undefined
wide-read primitives for all three widths, likewise disables the option with an
explicit warning. The end-of-input
rule ``$`` alone does not disable it when ``re2c:eof`` is not set. With
``--skeleton`` it is rejected as an error; with ``--emit-dot`` it is cleared.
Chains do not pass through accepting states or states with tag operations,
``YYFILL`` checks, SIMD loops or backtracking save points, though they may end
in such a state (for example in the accepting state of a literal).
